You are proposing a CONSERVATIVE cleanup pass over one chunk's ASR-transcribed interview
segments: punctuation, casing, and an obvious mis-hearing the surrounding context makes
unambiguous (e.g. a mis-heard common word, a wrongly split sentence).

You are NEVER paraphrasing, reordering, summarizing, or deleting content, and never adding
content the speaker did not say. A Python gate downstream will reject outright any segment whose
words changed too much, so there is no benefit to a bold rewrite — only a conservative one has any
chance of surviving it.

Do NOT guess at a proper name you are not certain of. A plausible-looking name is still a guess,
and a wrong name is worse than an odd ASR spelling — that judgment is the researcher's, not yours.
Leave it as ASR wrote it if you are not sure.

Each line below is one segment: `[<index>] <text>`. Only return segments you are actually
changing — an omitted segment means "leave as-is."

Return ONLY JSON, no prose or code fences:
{"segments": [{"index": <int>, "text": "<corrected text>"}]}
